    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d714">

                <pb facs="1000" n="990" xml:id="pg990"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6–1446: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Embassy in China
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-07-03T20:00:00-04:00">July 3, 1946—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>408. For Adler from the Secretary of the Treasury. As you know, cotton purchases
                    made (Reurtel 981, June 14) with funds accrued since Dec 7, 1945 are licensed by
                    General License 94. Practically all trade transactions financed by blocked
                    Chinese funds consummated under blanket license. To single out one commodity
                    category for special action seems unfeasible from funds control standpoint and
                    likely to engender very substantial objection from US cotton interests.
                    [Snyder.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
